Common shares carry residual ownership; preference rights vary; depositary receipts represent underlying securities through an intermediary.|Preferred holders may receive distributions before common holders under agreed terms.|Every preference share has identical voting rights?|No|Yes|Only if listed
Primary markets issue new securities; secondary markets trade existing claims.|A new share issue funds the company; an investor-to-investor sale usually does not.|Which directly funds the issuer?|A new issuance|An ordinary secondary trade|A change in quoted price
IPOs introduce public issuance; follow-ons, rights, and private placements raise additional capital under different processes.|A rights issue offers eligible existing owners subscription opportunities.|New issuance may dilute non-participating owners?|Yes|No|Only if profitable
Exchanges facilitate trading; indices summarise selected securities; market cap equals price times shares.|₹20 price × 10 shares gives ₹200 market cap.|Market cap is enterprise value automatically?|No|Yes|Only for banks
Free float excludes restricted or strategic holdings under index methodology; weighting rules affect index exposure.|Price ₹10, 100 shares, 40% eligible float gives ₹400 float-adjusted cap.|Float-adjusted cap is…?|₹400|₹1,000|₹40
Dividends distribute value; splits and bonus shares change units; buybacks reduce shares and cash.|A two-for-one split doubles shares and approximately halves per-share price absent other changes.|A split alone doubles total business value?|No|Yes|Only for liquid stocks
Market orders prioritise execution; limit orders constrain price but may not fill.|A buy limit of ₹100 is not executed above ₹100 under ordinary conditions.|Which risk remains?|Non-execution|Unlimited execution price|Guaranteed profit
Margin trading uses financing; short selling borrows and sells securities expecting later repurchase.|Short at ₹100 and cover at ₹130 loses ₹30 before costs.|Short-sale loss is inherently capped at the sale proceeds?|No|Yes|Only for large stocks
Securities lending temporarily transfers securities under contractual collateral and return terms.|A borrower uses lent shares to deliver a short sale.|The lender should assess…?|Counterparty, collateral, and recall terms|Only dividend yield|Only stock popularity
Fundamental analysis links business economics, financial statements, risk, and valuation.|Strong sales can coexist with weak cash generation and expensive pricing.|A good company is always a good investment at any price?|No|Yes|Only long term
Industry analysis examines competition, customers, suppliers, entry barriers, and substitutes.|A pricing advantage erodes when switching costs decline.|Competitive advantage must be assessed for…?|Durability and cash consequences|Only current margin|Only market size
An investment thesis states expected value drivers; catalysts affect recognition; risks can invalidate assumptions.|A margin-recovery thesis depends on pricing and input costs.|A useful thesis specifies…?|Disconfirming evidence and risks|Only target price|Only enthusiasm
Earnings surprises compare results with expectations, not just last year.|EPS ₹12 versus expectation ₹15 is a negative surprise despite prior EPS ₹10.|Year-on-year growth here is a positive earnings surprise?|No|Yes|Only if 20%
Research reports connect evidence, forecasts, valuation, risks, and disclosures.|A price target is supported by explicit operating and discount-rate assumptions.|What makes it reviewable?|Traceable assumptions and sources|Only a recommendation|Only a chart
Value, growth, quality, momentum, and income styles emphasise different characteristics and risks.|A dividend strategy can concentrate in mature sectors.|Style labels guarantee superior returns?|No|Yes|Only over long horizons
Technical analysis studies price and volume patterns; validation must account for costs, changing regimes, and selection bias.|A chart signal works in-sample but fails after trading costs.|Evidence should prioritise…?|Out-of-sample net performance|Only attractive chart patterns|Only gross backtest returns
Ownership concentration and pledged shares can affect governance and forced-sale risk.|Falling prices trigger collateral calls against an owner's pledged shares.|A pledge can create…?|Financing and control-related risk|New company revenue|Guaranteed liquidity
